1. Why async/await exists

Async programming allows applications to perform non-blocking operations, especially for I/O tasks like:
	•	Database calls
	•	HTTP calls
	•	File operations

Without async:
Thread → waits for DB
Thread → blocked

With async:
Thread → start DB call
Thread → returned to thread pool
DB completes → continuation runs

Async does not make code faster. It improves scalability and throughput.

2. Task (Foundation of async)

In .NET async operations return Task.

A Task represents future work.

Task types

Task - No return value.
Task SaveDataAsync()
Task - Returns a value later.
Task<int> GetUserIdAsync()

3. async keyword

async does not make the method asynchronous.

It only allows use of await.

Example:
public async Task<int> GetDataAsync()
{
    var data = await GetFromDbAsync();
    return data;
}

4. await keyword

await means:

Pause the method until the Task completes without blocking the thread.

Flow:
Start method
   |
await async operation
   |
Thread released
   |
Task completes
   |
Method resumes

5. What the compiler does (Async State Machine)

The compiler converts async methods into a state machine.

Conceptually:
state = 0 → start task
state = 1 → resume after await

The compiler generates something like:
MoveNext()
{
   if(state == 0)
   {
       start task
       if(not complete)
       {
           save state
           return
       }
   }

   if(state == 1)
   {
       continue execution
   }
}

Async methods are compiled into state machines with continuations

6. SynchronizationContext

SynchronizationContext decides:

Which thread runs the continuation after await

Example:
await GetDataAsync();
UpdateUI();

Console App = ThreadPool
ASP.NET Core = ThreadPool
WPF/WinForms = UI Thread

UI thread
   |
await API call
   |
UI thread released
   |
Task completes
   |
Continuation posted to UI thread

7. ConfigureAwait(false)

await GetDataAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);

Meaning:

Do not capture SynchronizationContext.

Benefits:
	•	avoids deadlocks
	•	improves performance
	•	used in library code

Important:
ConfigureAwait(false)
ONLY affects SynchronizationContext

8. ExecutionContext

ExecutionContext flows runtime environment data across threads.

It carries:
	•	AsyncLocal values
	•	Security context
	•	Culture
	•	Logical call context

Example:
AsyncLocal<string> user = new AsyncLocal<string>();
user.Value = "Admin";

Even if code runs on another thread:
Task.Run(() => Console.WriteLine(user.Value));
Output still:
Admin
Because ExecutionContext flows the value.


9. ExecutionContext vs SynchronizationContext
ExecutionContext
  - Purpose
    - Flows Data 
  - Handles
    - AsyncLocal, security, culture
  - Concerns
    - data flow

SynchornizationContext
  - Purpose
    - Controls Thread
  - Handles
    - continuation scheduling
  - Concerns
    - thread affinity

ExecutionContext = backpack (data)
SynchronizationContext = GPS (where to run)

10. Task.WhenAll

Used for parallel async execution.

Sequential:
await A();
await B();
await C();

Time:
A + B + C

Parallel:
var t1 = A();
var t2 = B();
var t3 = C();

await Task.WhenAll(t1,t2,t3);
Time:
max(A,B,C)

Much faster for independent tasks.

11. async void vs async Task

Bad:
async void Method()

Good:
async Task Method()
Reason:
	•	cannot await async void
	•	exceptions crash process

async void is used only for event handlers

12. Deadlock scenario

Bad code:
var result = GetDataAsync().Result;
Flow:
Main thread waits
Async continuation needs same thread
Thread blocked
Deadlock

Solution:
await GetDataAsync();

13. Important Internal Flow

When await happens:
1 Capture ExecutionContext
2 Capture SynchronizationContext
3 Run async operation
4 Task completes
5 Restore ExecutionContext
6 Schedule continuation using SynchronizationContext

14. Thread usage in async

Important:
async/await does NOT create new threads
Threads are used only when needed.

Example I/O flow:
await HTTP call
   |
OS handles request
   |
Completion callback
   |
ThreadPool executes continuation

15. Complete Mental Model
Task = future work
async = enables await
await = pause method without blocking thread
Compiler = creates state machine
ExecutionContext = carries data
SynchronizationContext = decides continuation thread
Task.WhenAll = parallel async execution

Async/await in C# is used for writing non-blocking asynchronous code. 
Async methods return Task or Task, and when an await is encountered the method pauses without blocking the thread. 
The compiler converts async methods into state machines that resume execution when the awaited task completes.
ExecutionContext flows runtime data such as AsyncLocal values across threads, 
while SynchronizationContext determines where continuations run, such as returning to the UI thread in desktop applications.
